# Dev workflow: default `docker compose` to the base + dev override so the
# barrelman API runs with hot-reload (bun --hot, source mounted) — code changes
# in src/ apply instantly, no image rebuild. Comment out for a prod-style run.
COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.dev.yml

# Database
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://barrelman:barrelman@localhost:5434/barrelman
BARRELMAN_DB_PASSWORD=barrelman

# Interface the internal services publish on: Postgres (5434), Martin (5002),
# GraphHopper (5003), MOTIS (5004). None of them authenticate, so they bind to
# loopback and only the barrelman API (5001) is reachable from off-box. Set to
# 0.0.0.0 only if you intend to expose them and have a firewall in front.
# BARRELMAN_BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1

# ── Postgres tuning ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The stock postgres image ships shared_buffers=128MB, which for a 23GB
# geo_places means nearly every search projection is a cold read — it was the
# difference between a 6.6s and a 0.2s FTS layer on an uncached query.
#
# Defaults in docker-compose.yml are sized for a dev laptop sharing RAM with
# MOTIS, Elasticsearch and GraphHopper: 1GB shared_buffers inside a 2g cap.
# Production should give the database the box — roughly 25% of host RAM in
# shared_buffers and 50-75% in effective_cache_size, e.g. on a 32GB host:
# BARRELMAN_DB_SHARED_BUFFERS=8GB
# BARRELMAN_DB_CACHE_SIZE=24GB
# BARRELMAN_DB_WORK_MEM=128MB
# BARRELMAN_DB_MAINTENANCE_WORK_MEM=2GB
# BARRELMAN_DB_MEM_LIMIT=28g
#
# mem_limit must cover shared_buffers + shm_size + maintenance_work_mem, with
# headroom. shared_buffers and /dev/shm are both shared memory charged to the
# same cgroup, so they add up — exceed the limit and the kernel OOM-kills
# Postgres mid-index-build, which surfaces only as "connection to server was
# lost". Check with: docker inspect barrelman-db --format '{{.State.OOMKilled}}'
#
# /dev/shm holds parallel index-build memory. Docker's 64MB default is too
# small ("could not resize shared memory segment ... No space left on device").
# Raise it alongside maintenance_work_mem.
# BARRELMAN_DB_SHM_SIZE=1gb
#
# random_page_cost defaults to 1.1 (SSD/NVMe). Raise toward 4 only on spinning
# disks, where it stops the planner from over-preferring index scans.
# BARRELMAN_DB_RANDOM_PAGE_COST=1.1

# Statement timeout (ms) for the API query pool. Nothing cancels the queries
# behind abandoned autocomplete keystrokes — postgres-js has no cancellation
# and the pool is 10 connections — so this stops one slow query from starving
# the pool. Schema DDL and the enrichment backfill are exempt (src/db.ts).
# 0 disables it.
# BARRELMAN_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# Server
# In Docker (docker-compose.yml), PORT is hardcoded to 5001.
# In local dev without Docker, use the same port or any available port.
PORT=5001

# The origin /docs advertises as its "Production" server, so the docs'
# try-it button targets the real API. Unset, only localhost is offered.
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://api.barrelman.dev

# Auth (simple shared secret — change before any public/production deployment)
BARRELMAN_API_KEY=brm_dev_changeme

# Admin access is account-based — there is no shared admin secret.
#
# The console at /console is reached by signing in, and the FIRST account
# created on a fresh instance becomes an administrator, so a new deployment is
# never locked out. For scripts and CI, an admin creates an API key carrying the
# `admin` scope: unlike the BARRELMAN_ADMIN_KEY it replaces, that key has an
# owner in the audit log, an optional expiry, and revokes instantly.

# Ollama (for semantic embeddings — optional)
OLLAMA_HOST=http://localhost:11434

# ── Region selection (unified) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Single source of truth for which regions the whole pipeline imports — OSM,
# GTFS, GBFS, and the Pelias geocoder all resolve from it.
# Comma-separated region keys for dev, or "global" for prod.
#   dev (default): north-carolina,nyc-metro
#   prod:          global
#
# Read by the importers, which run in barrelman-ops — not by the API. Recreate
# that service after changing it: docker compose up -d barrelman-ops
#
# To add a region by name (e.g. Colorado), cache the boundary catalog once:
#   docker compose exec barrelman-ops bun run scripts/fetch-boundaries.ts
# then create it in the admin console (Regions → Add by name) — the download
# URLs, bbox, transit search area and address sources are filled in for you.
# Definitions live in the import_regions table, seeded from config/regions.json.
# Full guide: docs/REGIONS.md
REGIONS=north-carolina,nyc-metro

# Optional. Raises GitHub's 60-request/hour anonymous API limit, used when
# resolving which OpenAddresses files cover a newly added region.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=

# ── OSM Import & Daily Updates ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Legacy single-extract override. When set, it bypasses REGIONS for run-import.sh
# (and update-osm.sh full mode). Normally leave unset and use REGIONS above.
# Find your region at https://download.geofabrik.de
# GEOFABRIK_URL=https://download.geofabrik.de/north-america/us/north-carolina-latest.osm.pbf

# Replication server URL. Used by update-osm.sh (replication mode) and init-replication.sh.
# Typically the extract URL with "-updates/" appended.
GEOFABRIK_REPLICATION_URL=https://download.geofabrik.de/north-america/us/north-carolina-updates/

# Update mode for update-osm.sh: "replication" (incremental diffs) or "full" (re-download + re-import)
# "replication" is faster; "full" is simpler and always consistent.
# Requires init-replication.sh to be run once before using "replication" mode.
UPDATE_MODE=replication

# ── GTFS Transit Data ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GTFS feeds are resolved from REGIONS above (each region's gtfsRegion). That is
# a Transitland feed *search area*: a "west,south,east,north" bounding box, the
# literal "global" (all ~2,800 feeds), or one of the legacy named tokens
# (nc, nyc, southeast, us). Anything else is rejected — it used to fall through
# to an unfiltered query and quietly download the whole global catalog.
# Set GTFS_REGION only to override every region with a single value.
# GTFS_REGION=-109.06,36.99,-102.04,41

# Transitland API key (get one at https://www.transit.land/users/sign_up)
TRANSITLAND_API_KEY=

# MOTIS transit router URL.
# In docker-compose this is set automatically via service networking.
MOTIS_URL=http://localhost:8080

# How often (seconds) MOTIS re-polls every feed's GTFS-RT URLs. Baked into the
# generated motis/config.yml (timetable.update_interval) by the GTFS import /
# `bun run import/generate-motis-config.ts`. MOTIS default is 60s, which means
# continuously fetching realtime for every agency. On a dev box that only
# occasionally exercises transit, raise this (e.g. 600) to cut the polling while
# keeping realtime data present and testable. Leave unset for prod.
# MOTIS_RT_UPDATE_INTERVAL=600

# ── GraphHopper Routing Engine ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Upstream URL for the GraphHopper container. Used by the /graphhopper proxy in
# the barrelman API. In docker-compose this is set automatically; override here
# for local dev (e.g. when running `bun run dev` against a containerised GH).
GRAPHHOPPER_URL=http://localhost:8989

# JVM options for GraphHopper (memory, GC, etc.)
#
# The heap must fit the whole routing graph — GraphHopper loads graph-cache into
# heap, it does not memory-map it. A north-carolina+nyc-metro graph-cache is
# ~2.5GB on disk (the three landmark files are ~544MB each), and -Xmx2g dies
# ~9s into startup with an OutOfMemoryError thrown from the JVM's uncaught
# exception handler — before it logs its version, so the failure looks like a
# crash rather than a memory problem. Size this above `du -sh` of graph-cache,
# and raise it whenever REGIONS grows.
GRAPHHOPPER_JAVA_OPTS="-Xmx5g -Xms1g"

# How many GraphHopper isochrone requests /isochrone issues in parallel. A
# transit isochrone fans out to hundreds of per-stop walk isochrones, but
# GraphHopper saturates its own thread pool well before the client runs out of
# slots, and the same engine serves interactive /route traffic. Raise only if
# GraphHopper is dedicated to isochrone work.
# ISOCHRONE_CONCURRENCY=8

# ── Tile server and geocoder ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both default to the compose service names, so they only need setting to point
# barrelman at an instance outside this stack.
# MARTIN_URL=http://barrelman-martin:3000
# PELIAS_URL=http://pelias_api:4000

# Where the API reads GTFS ZIPs from (for /transit/shapes and vehicle lookups).
# Compose mounts ./data/gtfs at /gtfs-zips and sets this accordingly.
# GTFS_DATA_DIR=./data/gtfs

# Set to "production" on a deployed instance. The server warns at startup about
# missing secrets when it is set.
# NODE_ENV=production

# ── Public API accounts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Barrelman is a public API: users sign in to the console, mint their own API
# keys, and are billed for metered usage. These settings cover sign-in itself;
# see the billing block below for Polar.

# Public origin of this API. Used in verification emails and as the OAuth
# redirect base, so it must match what a browser actually reaches.
# BARRELMAN_SERVER_ORIGIN=https://api.barrelman.dev

# Origin the console is served from. Defaults to the API origin, since the API
# serves the console at /console. Set this only if you host the console
# separately — it also determines the WebAuthn relying-party ID for passkeys.
# BARRELMAN_CONSOLE_ORIGIN=https://console.barrelman.dev

# Extra browser origins allowed to present a session cookie (comma-separated).
# BARRELMAN_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://staging.barrelman.dev

# "open" (default) lets anyone sign up. "invite" restricts sign-in to accounts
# an administrator has already created.
# BARRELMAN_REGISTRATION_MODE=open

# The first account created on a fresh instance becomes an administrator.
# Further admins are promoted from the console (Users -> make administrator);
# the last one cannot be demoted, so an instance always has someone who can
# administer it.

# Session lifetime and one-time-code lifetime.
# BARRELMAN_SESSION_TTL_DAYS=30
# BARRELMAN_OTP_TTL_MINUTES=15

# New accounts one source address may create per day. 0 disables the limit.
# BARRELMAN_SIGNUPS_PER_IP_PER_DAY=5

# Set to false to disable accounts entirely and fall back to the legacy
# shared-secret auth above.
# BARRELMAN_ACCOUNTS_ENABLED=true

# ── Email delivery (sign-in codes) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Without SMTP configured, sign-in codes are printed to the server log instead
# of sent — fine for development, not for a public deployment.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# SMTP_PORT=465
# SMTP_SECURE=true
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASS=
# SMTP_FROM=Barrelman <noreply@barrelman.dev>

# ── OAuth sign-in ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each provider turns on as soon as both of its variables are set. Register the
# redirect URI as <BARRELMAN_SERVER_ORIGIN>/auth/oauth/<provider>/callback.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GITLAB_CLIENT_ID=
# GITLAB_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GITLAB_BASE_URL=https://gitlab.com

# ── Licensing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Signed token unlocking gated features. Only `billing` is gated today, and only
# the official deployment holds a license for it: the Commons Clause in LICENSE
# removes the right to sell barrelman, so a self-hosted instance may not charge
# third parties for access. See LICENSING.md.
#
# Everything else — search, tiles, routing, transit, accounts, keys, metering —
# needs no license and never will.
# BARRELMAN_LICENSE=
# Verification key for the token above. The real one is compiled into
# src/lib/license.ts; set this only to verify a token signed by a different
# keypair.
# BARRELMAN_LICENSE_PUBLIC_KEY=

# ── Billing (Polar) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Requires BARRELMAN_LICENSE above. Without it these do nothing: the server logs
# a warning, every account sits on the free plan, the console hides its billing
# pages, and the Polar SDK is never constructed. On a self-hosted instance
# metering exists only to show you your own usage.
# POLAR_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# POLAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# POLAR_ORGANIZATION_ID=
# POLAR_SANDBOX=true

# Polar product IDs for the paid plans (see src/billing/plans.ts).
# POLAR_DEVELOPER_PRODUCT_ID=
# POLAR_SCALE_PRODUCT_ID=

# One-off credit packs, as productId:credits pairs. Purchased credits never
# expire and are spent only after the monthly allowance is used up.
# POLAR_CREDIT_PACKS=prod_abc:100000,prod_def:1000000

# Meter slug that overage events are ingested under. Must match the meter
# configured in Polar, or events are accepted and then billed by nothing.
# POLAR_USAGE_EVENT_NAME=barrelman_credits
# POLAR_METER_USAGE=true

# ── Metering internals ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Usage counters are buffered in memory and flushed on this interval. A crash
# loses at most one interval of counters, always in the customer's favour.
# BARRELMAN_USAGE_FLUSH_MS=5000
# How long a credit balance is cached before being re-read. An account can
# overshoot by at most the traffic it lands inside this window.
# BARRELMAN_BALANCE_CACHE_MS=15000
# How often metered overage is batched to the billing provider.
# BARRELMAN_OVERAGE_REPORT_MS=900000

# ── Abuse controls ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Layered throttling (see src/services/throttle.service.ts). All in-memory, so
# with N API replicas the effective limits are N times these.

# How many reverse proxies sit in front of barrelman. Every per-address limit
# below depends on this: X-Forwarded-For is a list whose first entry the caller
# writes, so the address is counted in from the right to land on the entry your
# own proxy observed. 0 ignores the header entirely and uses the peer address
# (barrelman exposed directly); 1 for one reverse proxy; 2 behind a CDN too.
# Too high lets callers spoof past the limits; too low puts everyone in one
# bucket. Both fail quietly.
# BARRELMAN_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1

# Requests per minute from one address when nobody is authenticated. Only
# reachable in an open deployment, where it is the only thing between a scraper
# and the database.
# BARRELMAN_ANON_RPM=120
# Ceiling on any single source address regardless of credentials. Generous —
# corporate NAT and mobile carriers put many real users behind one address.
# BARRELMAN_IP_RPM=3000
# Share of an account's per-minute budget a single key may spend, so one leaked
# key cannot starve the account's other keys.
# BARRELMAN_PER_KEY_SHARE=0.8

# Simultaneous in-flight requests per account for endpoints that occupy an
# upstream engine. Without these, a caller inside their per-minute limit can
# still pin every GraphHopper worker.
# BARRELMAN_ISOCHRONE_CONCURRENCY_PER_ACCOUNT=2
# BARRELMAN_TRANSIT_CONCURRENCY_PER_ACCOUNT=4
# BARRELMAN_ROUTING_CONCURRENCY_PER_ACCOUNT=8

# Penalty box: rejections tolerated before a caller is refused outright for an
# escalating period. Stops key-guessers and clients wedged in a retry loop.
# BARRELMAN_ABUSE_STRIKES=25
# Strikes before an abuse signal is raised for a human to review.
# BARRELMAN_ABUSE_SIGNAL_STRIKES=100

# Automated detection, run on the periodic sweep.
# Daily spend as a multiple of the plan's MONTHLY allowance before flagging.
# BARRELMAN_BURN_RATE_MULTIPLE=3
# Multiple at which a paid account is automatically held. Only applies where
# overage is allowed — a plan without overage stops itself at the allowance.
# BARRELMAN_BURN_RATE_SUSPEND_MULTIPLE=25
# How long an automatic hold lasts before lifting itself.
# BARRELMAN_AUTO_SUSPEND_HOURS=6
# Accounts from one sign-up address before it is flagged for review. Only ever
# a signal — a university or office NAT trips this legitimately.
# BARRELMAN_MULTI_ACCOUNT_THRESHOLD=6

# How often expired sessions are swept, suspensions expired and detection run.
# BARRELMAN_ACCOUNT_SWEEP_MS=3600000

# ── Scheduled jobs ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Recurring imports (OSM replication, GTFS drift, GBFS) are defined in the
# console under Schedules and stored in the database — there is nothing to
# configure here to use them. These two only affect how the scheduler behaves.
#
# Timezone offered as the default when creating a schedule. Any IANA zone; each
# schedule stores its own, so changing this never moves an existing one.
# BARRELMAN_SCHEDULE_TZ=UTC
# How often the API checks for due schedules. Cron has minute granularity, so
# there is nothing to gain below 30s. Floor is 10s.
# BARRELMAN_SCHEDULER_INTERVAL_MS=30000

# ── Terms of service ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Setting a URL turns on acceptance: users must agree before creating an API
# key. Bump the version when the terms materially change and everyone is asked
# again — existing keys keep working, so a bump never breaks an integration.
# BARRELMAN_TOS_URL=https://barrelman.dev/terms
# BARRELMAN_TOS_VERSION=1
# BARRELMAN_PRIVACY_URL=https://barrelman.dev/privacy

# Business plan product (added alongside Developer and Scale).
# POLAR_BUSINESS_PRODUCT_ID=

# ── Landing-site demo (docker-compose.dev.yml, `landing` profile) ──────────────
# The interactive demo in the hero on barrelman.dev. Both are keys on an account
# an administrator has moved onto the `demo` plan, which serves the API without
# spending credits and bounds each visitor separately — see docs/accounts.md.
#
# Ordinary keys on an ordinary account, deliberately: the demo is revocable like
# any other consumer, and revoking it cannot take Parchment down with it. Do not
# reuse BARRELMAN_API_KEY here.

# Server-side, for /api/demo/*. Never reaches the browser.
# BARRELMAN_DEMO_KEY=brm_live_...

# Browser-side, for the map tiles MapLibre fetches itself. This one is public by
# necessity — it ships in the page — so scope it to `tiles` and nothing else,
# and keep it separate from the key above so either can be revoked alone.
# BARRELMAN_TILE_DEMO_KEY=brm_live_...
